---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';
const app = appBySlug('yotpo-email-sms')!;
const path = '/apps/yotpo-email-sms';
const title = 'Yotpo Email & SMS: migration and retained products';
const desc = 'This historical messaging route is a migration record. Preserve permissions and messaging dependencies while keeping Reviews and Loyalty decisions separate.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">← App procurement directory</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">Operating review · checked November 25, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <article class="container prose-shopify pb-16">

    <h2>Messaging retirement changes the buying decision</h2>
    <p><a class="link" href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">Yotpo announced full Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025</a>. As of this review in November 2026, this route should not be used to shortlist a new Yotpo messaging deployment. Yotpo continues to present Reviews and Loyalty products; retiring messaging does not mean those products must be removed.</p>
    <p>The official migration article names Attentive and Omnisend as partners. That is vendor migration guidance, not an independent ranking or a guarantee that every old flow transfers. Confirm current account access and assistance with the relevant vendor before planning around an old migration offer.</p>
    <h2>Start with the evidence you still have</h2>
    <p>Inventory existing exports, account notices, invoices, integration configurations and destination-platform records. If source access is unavailable, identify the missing fields and ask support what can still be recovered. Do not assume historical exports remain obtainable indefinitely or rebuild permission evidence from a subscriber count.</p>
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Asset</th><th>Reconciliation task</th><th>Do not infer</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>SMS and email permissions</td><td>Match channel, status, source and available capture evidence to destination records</td><td>A contact row means permission for every channel</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Suppressions</td><td>Import exclusions before enabling sending audiences</td><td>An absent source opt-out can be replaced with a default subscribed value</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sending numbers and keywords</td><td>Confirm ownership, transfer status, registration and reply routing with the new provider</td><td>A phone-number export transfers the sending identity</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Templates and flows</td><td>Rebuild triggers, waits, branches, links and exits</td><td>Copied message text preserves execution behavior</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Reporting history</td><td>Archive definitions and attribution windows beside exported totals</td><td>Old and new attributed revenue are directly comparable</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <h2>Keep Reviews and Loyalty attached to their jobs</h2>
    <p>List the storefront widgets, review requests, loyalty balances, referral links and rewards used by your store. Assign each to the product or integration that currently owns it. A messaging replacement should not accidentally remove a review widget or interrupt reward redemption merely because the old sender is being disconnected.</p>
    <p>For every message containing loyalty data, map the customer identifier, balance field, reward link and update timing. For review requests, decide which system sends the request and which system records the resulting review. This avoids two request messages for the same order when a replacement ESP and a retained Yotpo product both have a trigger enabled.</p>
    <h2>Proposed integration rehearsal</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Create a test customer with known email and SMS permissions and a recorded loyalty state.</li>
      <li>Place a test order and inspect the identifiers passed into the replacement sender.</li>
      <li>Check that the message displays the intended reward or review destination for that customer.</li>
      <li>Change the loyalty state or refund the order, then inspect any queued follow-up for stale content.</li>
      <li>Opt out of SMS and verify suppression while checking the separately recorded email preference.</li>
      <li>Confirm only the assigned system sends the review request and record the final queue state.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>This is an acceptance proposal, not a reported integration test. Where a connector does not provide a field or timely event, document the gap and change the journey rather than filling the template with assumed data.</p>
    <h2>Cutover or repair an incomplete migration</h2>
    <p>If you already moved, audit the destination rather than restarting the entire project. Compare permission and suppression totals by status, investigate unmatched identities, and inspect a sample of capture records. Check live forms, checkout settings and old links so newly captured permissions reach the system that now sends.</p>
    <p>If the migration is incomplete, separate recovery tasks from new campaign launches. Pause flows with uncertain audience eligibility, obtain the best available source records, and configure a single active sender for each job. Ask the destination provider how queued contacts are handled during import. Do not schedule parallel campaigns merely to compare reported revenue; that can duplicate contact and distort the comparison.</p>
    <h2>Evaluate the replacement on retained dependencies</h2>
    <p>Ask shortlisted providers to demonstrate the specific Reviews or Loyalty integration you use, including field availability and failure handling. Get a current quote for sends, contacts, SMS destinations, implementation and integration work. An old migration incentive does not establish current pricing or service availability.</p>
    <p>Keep a signed-off handover record with the sending owner, suppression reconciliation, domain authentication, number-transfer outcome, active flows and retained-product settings. Archive historical attribution separately so a reporting reset is not misread as a change in customer demand.</p>
    <h2>Common migration questions</h2>
    <p><strong>Is SMSBump a separate new alternative?</strong> Treat the <a class="link" href="/apps/smsbump">historical SMSBump route</a> as part of the legacy messaging investigation, not a second current messaging purchase.</p>
    <p><strong>Must Reviews and Loyalty move too?</strong> Make that decision separately. First establish what your retained products still own and what the replacement messaging integration must consume.</p>

    <h2>Catalog acceptance brief</h2>
    <p>{app.shopifyFit}</p>
    <p>This is a proposed evaluation, not an observed merchant test or a claim that the platform passed it. Save the evidence, unresolved exceptions and responsible owner before approving a change.</p>
    <h2>Continue the decision</h2>
    <p>Return to the <a class="link" href="/apps">procurement directory</a> to compare operating requirements. Use the <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing worksheet</a> for your actual quote and the <a class="link" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">channel coordination guide</a> when more than one sender remains active.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
